{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01BBSB6SYfSpr4fZJsyjT6Yk","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01UGoAyd7q7QaAsfyraLABDY","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0513/projects/collapse_aware_reward_rebalancing/docs/paper/abstract.tex"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":496,"cache_read_input_tokens":18579,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":496,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":53,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_015pjhtxNKRtK76QMrtqLQuN","session_id":"84f80a2f-4345-4e37-b08e-533f31b43a49","uuid":"69ba3bd1-8781-4550-8a43-346137109eb7"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01UGoAyd7q7QaAsfyraLABDY","type":"tool_result","content":"1\tIterative self-training with reward curation, where a reward model selects the best candidate response at each generation for supervised fine-tuning, is widely expected to erode output diversity through compounding selection pressure. We test this expectation with a controlled factorial experiment spanning 8 configurations, 3 iterative generations, and both Low-Rank Adaptation (LoRA) and full fine-tuning. The central finding contradicts the prevailing concern: semantic diversity, measured by Vendi Score over sentence embeddings, remains within $\\pm$1\\% of its initial value across all curated conditions, even at a 6.25\\% retention rate (top-1-of-16) where Vendi Score changes by only $-0.19\\%$. However, this stability masks what we term \\emph{metric-dependent collapse}. Iterative self-training causes substantial format homogenization regardless of curation strategy, with opening unique ratio falling from 0.757 to 0.477 and near-duplicate start rates rising from 37.9\\% to 75.6\\% under matched $K{=}4$ conditions, while embedding-based metrics remain blind to this convergence. We further document a direction reversal: curation suppresses diversity under LoRA ($-3.40$ percentage points relative to a random baseline) but promotes it under full fine-tuning ($+0.63$ pp), a pattern that tracks parameter capacity rather than selection intensity. These results establish that reward curation does not cause semantic collapse in iterative self-training, that diversity audits relying solely on embedding-based measures miss significant structural homogenization, and that conclusions about curation effects depend critically on the training regime under which they are measured."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_015pjhtxNKRtK76QMrtqLQuN","session_id":"84f80a2f-4345-4e37-b08e-533f31b43a49","uuid":"e2b005a5-1143-458b-93a0-d282b07335b6","timestamp":"2026-05-17T14:37:31.371Z"}
